            en: "Chester Barnard, onetime president of New Jersey Bell Telephone, wrote in The Functions of the Executive (1938) of an organization as a system of people willing to cooperate toward a common purpose, swapping the machine image for a cooperative one. His acceptance theory of authority held that an order becomes effective only when the subordinate understands it, sees it as consistent with personal interest, and has the ability and will to carry it out—authority lands at the receiving end, not in the throat of the one who issues it. He assigned executives three functions: maintaining communication (the lines between people), securing individual services (recruiting and motivating them), and defining purpose (setting direction and giving meaning). The book founded the social-systems school and pre-seeded later ideas of empowering leadership, psychological safety and employee involvement.",
