            en: "In Theory Z (1981) the Japanese-American scholar William Ouchi compared American and Japanese firms, opening with two reference types: the American market form (Type A—short-term employment, rapid personalized evaluation and promotion, narrow career paths) and the Japanese accumulative form (Type J—long-term employment, slow rating and promotion, informal holistic control). Between them he posited the Type Z organization: comparatively stable employment to breed belonging, collective decision-making to buy consensus at the point of execution, and concern for the employee and their whole life. It echoed McGregor's moving past the X-Y dichotomy and, with later comparative work, warned that institutions must grow in their cultural soil—lifelong employment and consensus transplanted whole into high-mobility, short-cycle settings often misfire.",
