            en: "Mary Parker Follett, a Boston social worker and theorist active around the First World War, left essays gathered in Creative Experience (1924). She argued conflict need not be win-lose: besides domination and compromise there is integration—put each side's real want on the table and invent a third option neither anticipated, negotiating from needs rather than positions. She defined leadership as \"the art of solving the problem\" rather than power itself, distinguished \"power over\" from \"power with,\" and urged managers to command by expertise and the law of the situation rather than by rank. Her idea of the \"law of the situation\"—letting the rule, not a person, give orders—was reclaimed only half a century later by management by objectives and shared leadership, so she is honored today as a forerunner of organizational behavior.",
