            en: "Hammer and Champy launched the reengineering movement in the 1990s, observing that firms are sliced by function into railroad segments, each department minding only its short stretch while no one owns the end-to-end result — so a single job is handed across boundaries, queues and waits, with nobody answerable for whether it turns out well. Reengineering asks you to forget how the work is divided now and return to why we do it at all, redesigning around the whole process rather than the department, exploiting information technology to merge roles that ran in series and run steps in parallel, and daring order-of-magnitude, nonlinear targets instead of tweaking the old structure. It delivered real gains and real disasters — used as a euphemism for mass downsizing, and often too violent to stick — but its deepest legacy was to plant the process lens firmly into all later management practice.",
