            en: "Mission, vision and values form an organisation's supreme court, each on its own clock. The mission answers 'why do we exist' — for whom, doing what — and should read true in twenty years. The vision answers 'which future do we want': a concrete, recognisable scene a decade out, replaced each time one is realised. Values answer 'what lines we will not cross in the pursuit', and are tested most where profit clashes with conscience. Their use is not lobby decoration but deciding hard cases: when strategy or allocation deadlocks, return to three questions — does it serve the mission, move toward the vision, honour the values. The quality test is counterintuitive: wording any competitor could copy onto their wall is ornament. A good mission is concrete enough to veto profitable opportunities — when seven people died of cyanide-laced Tylenol in Chicago in 1982, Johnson & Johnson, on its creed's first line ('responsibility is to doctors, nurses, patients, mothers and fathers everywhere'), recalled thirty-one million bottles despite having no legal duty, swallowed over a hundred million dollars, and bought trust that outlived the scandal. Values that have never cost the company money are not values but posters; they become culture only after they have been used to turn down cash.",
