          detail: {
            zh: "SWOT 源自 1960 年代初斯坦福研究所的长期计划研究（常被归于汉弗莱，出处有争议），贡献是把内部的强/弱与外部机会/威胁摆进同一张四格，是战略讨论最便宜、最直观的开场。常见失败是把“填满格子”当完工：格子里是形容词不是证据——“品牌强”强过谁、对哪个客户强；四十条平铺不分主次，等于没有重点；弱点写成“还不够好”的客气话；外部两格三年没换过。正确用法三步：证据标准——优势要能回答“比对手与顾客的其他选项强在哪”，机会要写出“错过它会怎样”；交叉组合（TOWS）——用优势抓机会、补短板乘机会、以优势防威胁、避威胁弃短板，每类组合产出候选动作；最后排序并标注时点——没写“截至何时”的四格分析会风化。四格只负责把问题摆上桌面，桌面下的证据得靠 PESTEL、五力与价值链去收集。",
            en: "SWOT descends from Stanford Research Institute's long-range-planning work around 1960 (credited to Humphrey, though its provenance is contested), and its gift is a four-cell grid sorting internal strengths and weaknesses against external opportunities and threats — the cheapest, most intuitive opening of strategy talk. The standard failure is treating the filled grid as the job done: cells hold adjectives, not evidence ('strong brand' — stronger than whom, for which customers); forty flat items, hence nothing important; weaknesses euphemised as 'room for improvement'; the external boxes not refreshed in three years. Use it in three moves instead. Demand evidence: a strength enters only if it beats competitors and customers' alternatives, and an opportunity must state what happens to us if we miss it. Cross-combine (the TOWS matrix): strength riding opportunity, weakness patched to seize opportunity, strength deployed against threat, weakness-and-threat calling for retreat — each crossing yields candidate moves. Then rank and date-stamp: an undated four-cell analysis fossilises. The grid's job is to put questions on the table; the evidence under the table still has to come from PESTEL, five forces and the value chain.",
          },
          tags: [{ zh: "分析", en: "Analysis" }],
          related: ["pestel", "porter-five-forces"],
