            en: "Michael Porter's 1979 move was to upgrade competition from watching rivals to reading structure: whether an industry keeps earning depends not on how glossy it looks but on five forces tugging at profit — the threat of new entrants (are barriers high), substitutes (is there a cheaper answer waiting outside), buyer power (can customers squeeze price), supplier power (can the upstream squeeze you), and rivalry among incumbents (is it all-out price war). The stronger the five, the more returns get competed away. Its real use is not filling boxes but asking: which force quietly grew in the last two years, and where can barriers be deliberately raised? Strategy then means occupying a structurally favourable position, or changing the forces themselves — say by lifting switching costs or building barriers. The trap is treating the five as a static snapshot: platforms, open source and regulation keep recutting industry boundaries, so the answer of a decade ago may run in reverse today.",
