            en: "In Competitive Strategy (1980) Michael Porter argued that within any industry there are only three coherent positions from which to earn above-average returns over the long run: win on lower cost across the whole industry, command a premium through uniqueness, or concentrate on a segment narrow enough to serve better. The three pull against one another, and the trap is being \"stuck in the middle\"—neither a cost leader nor a differentiator, squeezed from both sides. Trade-offs are the soul of strategy; deciding what not to do matters as much as what to do, because a position that tries to please every buyer and every need makes its own activities undercut each other. In What Is Strategy? (1996) he pressed the point further: strategy is not operational effectiveness piled up—efficiency can be copied by any fast follower—while a distinctive, mutually reinforcing fit of activities is the part that resists copying.",
