            en: "C. K. Prahalad and Gary Hamel, in The Core Competence of the Corporation (Harvard Business Review, 1990), reached for a tree: end products are the leaves, core products the trunk, competencies the roots—Honda's small, precise engines grew motorcycles, cars, mowers and marine motors alike, while Texas Instruments' skill in fine calibration spanned chips, displays and machine tools. They faulted the portfolio analytics then in vogue for asking only whether a unit was attractive, never whether its capabilities fit, so diversification became a heap of unrelated businesses. A competency earns the label \"core\" by three tests: potential to spawn entry into many markets, rivals' difficulty in imitating it, and a perceptible contribution to the value the customer feels. The idea guided build-versus-buy on one side and, later, the sharper question of what to outsource and what to keep in hand.",
