            en: "\"Pirate metrics,\" a framework popularised by the investor Dave McClure, borrows five initials to split growth into the five rings of the user lifecycle — acquisition, activation, retention, revenue and referral — each insisting on its own quantifiable key metric and north-star threshold. Its edge is not memorising the letters but forcing founders to put a number on every ring: whether the day-one and day-thirty retention curve flattens, whether the viral coefficient clears one, how free converts to paid — then to find the leakiest ring fast. Retention acts as the truth serum for whether anyone genuinely loves the product, since acquisition can be bought with cash burn but retention cannot be faked; if the first four rings fail, the final revenue ring has nothing to rest on. It is at once a startup's growth dashboard and the shared language for explaining growth logic to investors.",
