            en: "Bruce Henderson created the matrix for the Boston Consulting Group in 1970: the vertical axis is market growth (whether to feed the unit cash), the horizontal is relative share (whether it generates cash). It sorts businesses into four boxes — Stars, high on both, to be invested in to hold position; Cash Cows, low-growth and high-share, mature money-printers to be milked to fund the others; Question Marks, high-growth and low-share, a bet on the future to fund if you read it right and drop otherwise; and Dogs, weak on both, usually to be shrunk or divested. It hands a diversified group a simple map of where money comes from and where it goes, lifting allocation above the horse-trading in which every unit gets its share. Its limits are plain: it takes share equals strength and growth equals attractiveness as axioms that do not always hold, which is why it is often paired with the GE nine-box, which swaps in industry attractiveness and competitive strength as the axes.",
