            en: "McKinsey's Three Horizons framework, refined by Baghai and colleagues from the late 1990s through 2004, splits a firm's growth into three layers that live on very different clocks. Horizon 1 is the cash core to optimise and defend, squeezing out today's profit through efficiency; Horizon 2 is the fast-rising new business that needs real investment and tending before it pays off; Horizon 3 is the highly uncertain long-term option or experiment that could reshape the whole game. The crux is not producing a three-year plan but giving each horizon its own metrics, cadence and owner — the core judged on cash and share, the growth business on new customers and unit economics, the ventures on learning and milestones — otherwise Horizon 1, with its overpowering short-term numbers, devours every resource, and by the time the core plateaus the firm finds it has no next card left to play.",
