            en: "Economies of scale are the fall in unit cost as output rises, because fixed inputs (plant, R&D, tooling, platform) spread thinner and purchasing gains bargaining power. This is a natural moat for heavy factories, software and platforms, and explains why a large player can push prices below what rivals can match, even tipping toward winner-takes-all. But there is a limit: past a certain size come diseconomies of scale — bloated layers, soaring cross-team communication cost and bureaucratic self-expansion eating back what was saved. It is kin to the learning and experience curve (more repetition, more mastery, less cost), yet big must be weighed against nimble: big is not strong, and a bloated giant is just as fragile. Lean production and modular design exist to have it both ways — the low cost of scale and the agility of a small team.",
