            en: "Raised by the management philosopher Charles Handy and applied to organisations by de Geus in The Living Company: the S-curve traces a rise from investment, take-off, acceleration, maturity to decline, and the most dangerous moment is the peak itself, when vested interest, the inertia of success and the thought “this will keep paying for a few more years” make people refuse to change — turning only once the curve has crested is usually too late. The second curve must therefore be seeded while the first is still climbing, resources are plentiful and cash flow can still fund it, even though it looks smaller and less worthwhile early on, splits attention, competes with the core for resources, and often triggers what Christensen called the innovator's dilemma. The defining test of leadership is whether, at the height of success with no alarm ringing, one dares to self-disrupt and resist the temptation to simply harvest the first curve — Kodak and Nokia failed by turning only at the top.",
