            en: "The basic organisational forms each trade something away. The functional structure groups people by specialty — marketing, R&D, production, finance — so skills deepen and scale is efficient, but it raises thick silo walls, slows cross-team work, and has each function lobbying for itself. The divisional structure cuts the firm into near-independent units by product or region, close to the market with clear ownership of profit and loss, at the cost of duplicated functions and scattered resources. The matrix has one person answer to both a function and a project: flexible, letting scarce experts be shared across many projects, yet it manufactures the two-boss problem for every subordinate and slides easily into power politics. Chandler's line \"structure follows strategy\" frames the choice: there is no best structure everywhere, only one that serves the strategy in hand — cost leadership pulls toward a centralised functional form, diverse and fast-shifting markets push toward decentralised divisions, and only work that must both share experts and span domains is worth the friction of a matrix.",
