            en: "How many reports one manager should carry has never had a universal number. The span of seven has circulated for nearly a century (traced to a general's battlefield observation), yet research keeps finding no stable link between any particular span and performance. The real variables are four: how standardised the work is, how competent the subordinates are, how much coordination the task demands, and how strong the information systems are. The first two set how much intervention a manager actually owes — a repetitive line and a research team are not in the same order of magnitude. The third is the hidden killer: where cross-unit negotiation is heavy, a wide span fills the manager with meetings instead of the shop floor. The fourth is most overlooked: systems cheapen watching, which is why after collaboration tools matured many firms held spans constant and still widened what they could carry. Price both failure modes: narrow spans multiply layers, lengthen chains, distort information and inflate overhead; wide spans swamp the manager, stall delegation and thin the promotion rungs. So diagnose where the manager's hours actually go before touching spans — if meetings already own the calendar, redrawing reporting lines only reroutes the jam.",
