            en: "Cutting layers and widening spans has been near-synonymous with reform since the nineties: shorter chains, faster decisions, closeness to the front line, with the information losses and ornamental privilege of hierarchy shaved away together. But flat was never flatter-is-better; it is arithmetic with hard constraints: span-of-control research keeps finding a ceiling on how many reports a manager can truly lead, and cutting two layers in one stroke so reporting lines jump from seven to thirty-five — without the systems and delegation to match — paralyses coordination instead. The real question is what standardisation can replace: work that is proceduralised never needed a watcher, and with good enough information systems a manager need not serve as a message relay, which is exactly why large firms run wide spans with few layers. The cost table must be laid out too: promotion rungs thin and titles inflate, and the people who absorb all this are the middle — their tacit experience, coaching and cross-department stitching is the line item cutters never see. The predictable failure mode wears flatness as a badge: the chart declares itself flat while every decision still crests five sign-offs, only now nobody owns one. The test has always been plain: after removing those two layers, does information still distort and decisions still queue? If not, the layers were spare; if worse, you have only moved the complexity into gaps no one governs.",
