          level: 2,
          oneLiner: {
            zh: "正式架构图之外，人还会自发结成小圈子、潜规则与舆论——它既能补位也能拖垮组织。",
            en: "Beyond the org chart people form cliques, unwritten norms and gossip — which can either shore up or sink an organisation.",
          },
          detail: {
            zh: "霍桑实验与巴纳德先后发现：企业里不只有写进制度的“正式组织”，还有一张基于情感、利益与习惯自然长出的“非正式组织”。它有看不见的规范（比如“别干太快，害得大家都难看”）、有意想不到的非正式领袖、以及比公告栏跑得更快的八卦网，实实在在地左右着士气与执行力。公开否认它、或硬碰硬地压它，常常失效——正式制度越是无视它，落地越容易走样。聪明的做法是先承认它存在，找出真正的非正式意见领袖、借他们的嘴传递意图，并尽量让潜规则与正式目标对齐，而不是相互拧着。它是一把理解“为什么好政策到了下面变了形”的钥匙：管组织，除了看流程图，还得读那张看不见的人际地图。",
            en: "Hawthorne and Barnard each found that a firm holds not only the formal organisation written into its rules but also an informal one grown naturally from feeling, interest and habit. It has unseen norms (do not work too fast and shame the rest), unexpected unofficial leaders, and a grapevine that outruns any noticeboard, and it genuinely sways morale and execution. Outright denial, or bulldozing it, usually backfires — the more formal policy ignores it, the more it lands distorted. The wise move is to acknowledge it first, locate the real informal opinion leaders and route intent through them, and bend the unwritten rules toward the stated goals rather than wrenching against them. It is a key to why good policy changes shape on its way down: to manage an organisation you must read not only the org chart but the invisible human map beneath it.",
          },
