            zh: "网络化组织是对大而全与层级臃肿的反动：企业把制造、物流甚至研发交给伙伴，自己守住品牌与设计，像耐克那样轻资产地调度一张供应与协作之网。它的经济学很直白：按科斯的问题，当外部交易成本低于内部组织成本，边界就该往外推；数字平台又把协调成本再压一层，于是“公司”与“市场”之间长出一大片中间形态——特许经营、战略联盟、平台生态。优点是弹性：需求突变时调得动资源，重资产风险被分摊给伙伴。代价同样清单化：控制力让渡给了合同与信任，关键节点失衡便牵一发而动全身——芯片断供把这条教训卖给了全世界；利润也须共享，伙伴拿走的正是你不再背的成本与风险。所以它真正的管理对象不是员工而是关系：选择标准、如何度绩效、怎样防机会主义，这三题答不好，网络化就只是外包的另一个说法。",
            en: "The network form is a reaction against bigness and bulky hierarchy: the firm hands manufacturing, logistics and even research to partners and keeps brand and design for itself, orchestrating a web of supply and collaboration asset-light, the way Nike has long done. The economics are plain. In Coase's question, boundaries move outward wherever external transaction costs fall below the cost of organising the same work inside; digital platforms compress coordination costs again, so a broad middle zone grows between firm and market — franchises, alliances, platform ecosystems. The merit is resilience: demand shocks can be absorbed by moving resources rather than re-tooling the plant, and heavy-asset risk is shared with partners. The bill is itemised too. Control is traded into contracts and trust, so imbalance at a critical node propagates everywhere — the chip shortages sold that lesson to the whole world; and profit must be shared, since what partners take is precisely the cost and risk you no longer carry. What the network form actually manages, then, is relationships rather than employees: which standards to impose, how performance is measured, how opportunism is deterred. Answer those three poorly and the network is just another word for outsourcing.",
