            en: "When conditions shift fast and escalation is too slow, organizations reweave the front line into teams that are cross-functional, goal-bound and self-managing, handing the team the planning and coordination once held by a supervisor. The evidence base is usually traced to 1951, when Trist and Bamforth, at Britain's Tavistock Institute, studied longwall coal mining: mechanization made fragmented jobs pointless, so miners formed interdependent, self-policing groups and both output and morale rose—giving rise to the socio-technical systems view. Team-based designs lift responsiveness, engagement and systemic understanding, but demand real collaboration and self-discipline; selection, training and incentives must then move from rewarding individuals to rewarding the team, or \"team\" stays a label while everyone still optimizes a private KPI.",
