            en: "Fiedler was the founder of the contingency approach to leadership. He gauged style with an odd instrument, the Least Preferred Coworker scale: asked to recall the hardest colleague they ever worked with, a leader who still describes that person in fairly positive terms is at bottom relationship-oriented, while one who speaks of them in wholly negative terms is task-oriented. He then judged how much control a situation gives the leader from three factors — leader-member relations, how structured the task is, and the leader's position power. The conclusion was counterintuitive: task-oriented leaders do best when the situation is either extremely favourable or extremely unfavourable, whereas relationship-oriented leaders prevail in the middle, the ambiguous stretch. More tellingly, Fiedler held that style is fairly fixed and hard to reshape on short notice, so rather than futilely trying to rewire the person you should match the right person to the right job, or reshape the situation to fit the leader you have.",
