            en: "Abraham Maslow, in 1943, drew human needs as a ladder—physiological, safety, love and belonging, esteem, and self-actualization (he later added cognitive, aesthetic and transcendent needs)—holding that people pursue the higher only once the lower are relatively met. Its management lesson is direct: those who are hungry or fearing the layoff can hardly be moved by slogans of self-actualization; leave the base needs of pay and security unsettled and every higher incentive is a castle in the air. It is also much criticized—thin empirical base, tiers too rigid, tinted with Western individualism, and in real life people create and keep dignity even amid poverty—so it is best read as a prompt rather than a precise model.",
