            en: "Around 1955 Herbert Simon argued in Administrative Behavior that real decision-makers have neither full information nor unlimited mind to exhaust every option and consequence, so they substitute satisficing for optimizing—set an acceptable threshold and take the first alternative that clears it rather than the theoretical optimum. March and Simon pushed this to the organization level in Organizations (1958): a coalition of boundedly rational members muddling through an \"organized anarchy.\" Simon won the 1978 Nobel in economics for this work. It spawns heuristics and framing: since reason is bounded, Kahneman and Tversky found people ride mental shortcuts and get pulled by how the same problem is worded; Thaler and Sunstein's \"nudge\" goes further—designing a better choice architecture beats blaming the individual.",
