            en: "The list of assumptions is long: clear and weighted goals, a complete and mutually exclusive set of options, information available without cost, outcomes estimable and rankable, and the highest expected value chosen. As a training framework it is worth its keep: spreading a decision across criteria, weights, options and scores forces hidden assumptions to speak, and converts disagreement from 'you don't get it' into 'we weight this criterion differently' — the cheapest reframe available in a meeting. But hold it as a ruler, not a map. In practice information is partial, time is short, criteria drift during the discussion itself, and options are usually manufactured rather than given; March's alternative is satisfaction, not optimisation, and goals get rewritten afterwards to make us look rational all along. Behavioural work adds two cuts: framing effects flip the choice by restating the same option, and anchors silently set the scores. So the working division is honest — heuristics for the small and frequent, this model to flatten the options on the rare and large, and bounded rationality to make the actual pick. Both are tools; nobody has to enlist.",
