            en: "A decision tree decomposes uncertainty into decision nodes, chance nodes and outcomes: a square for the choice you control, a circle for the probabilistic branches you leave to fate, leaves tagged with payoff and cost. Weighting each limb into an expected monetary value lets you compare options side by side and, pruning backward (backward induction), derive today's optimal path. Its worth is not accuracy but forcing hidden assumptions into the open—probabilities, payoffs, feasible actions all laid out to be challenged. The limits are hard too: probabilities are often eyeballed, and expected value ignores utility and ruin risk (a one-shot EV can hide a fatal downside), so read it alongside risk preference and sensitivity analysis. For multi-stage uncertainty it complements real options and Monte Carlo simulation.",
