            en: "Alex Osborn's 1953 rules were four: withhold judgement, welcome the far-fetched, go for volume, combine and improve — the mechanism being deferred criticism, so self-censorship does not kill an idea at birth. Seventy years of checking the ledger has been less charitable: meta-analyses across many experiments find that groups of the same size generate fewer and less original ideas than the same people writing silently and pooling afterwards. Three mechanisms explain it — social loafing (effort per person falls with headcount), production blocking (you queue to speak and forget by your turn), and evaluation anxiety with conformity (the loudest voice sets the tone early). So the modern use is not to abolish the session but to reorder it: generate independently first (nominal group, brainwriting), then sit together for judgement, combination and selection. Osborn's most misread rule is withholding criticism: it governs divergence only, and most meetings die of agreeableness precisely because they never converge.",
