            en: "De Bono's 1985 design turns six warring perspectives into a rota. White hat: facts and data — what do we know, what is missing. Red hat: feelings and intuition — stated plainly, needing no justification. Black hat: caution and risk — the devil's advocate made an office. Yellow hat: value and benefit — even if the vibe is negative, voice what works. Green hat: creativity and alternatives — criticism is banned here; its address is the black hat. Blue hat: process — worn by the chair, deciding which hat comes when. The soul is 'parallel thinking': at each moment the whole table looks the same way, separating generating from judging — the same root as brainstorming's deferred judgement. The gains are real: under the red hat an introvert's gut misgiving becomes legitimate; the black hat makes critique a duty rather than a personality; meetings shift from adversarial debate to joint prospecting. The limits too: in low-trust teams it decays into procedural theatre, and changing hats cannot invent missing information. Use it to run meetings, reviews and document walkthroughs — just do not expect the hats themselves to do the analysis.",
