            zh: "名义小组是一套把“产生点子”与“讨论点子”强行解耦的流程：静默各自书写（不得交谈）、轮流逐一陈述（每人一轮一次，此阶段不得评论）、澄清讨论（只能问是什么意思，不能判好坏）、最后匿名排序投票（常取 1–5 分）。它的强项恰好是头脑风暴的弱项：安静者不再被嗓门大者淹没，贡献与曝光分开，排名不记名因而难被权力劫持。它还有第二个常被忽略的身份：做优先级取舍时非常好用——当候选项多于能做的事，先各自打分再取总和，比听会议室里最自信的人更有效（1–9 分制的点投法就是它的变体）。代价也别隐藏：它比自由讨论更耗时间、也更容易把气氛弄得很“公事”，因而适合议题重、人多、地位差明显的场合；若目标只是暖场或建立共享感，那不如直接讨论。",
            en: "The nominal group technique is a procedure that forcibly uncouples generating ideas from discussing them: write silently and alone, round-robin statement (one contribution each turn, no comment yet), clarification discussion (questions of meaning only, not merit), then anonymous ranking by vote, commonly a one-to-five allocation. Its strengths are exactly brainstorming's weaknesses: the quiet are no longer drowned by the loud, contribution is separated from exposure, and rankings unattributed are hard for rank to capture. It has a second, less-remarked identity as a prioritisation tool — when there are more candidates than capacity, independent scoring summed beats whoever sounds most confident in the room (dot voting on a one-to-nine scale is its descendant). Do not hide the costs either: it is slower and more procedural than free discussion, which makes it right for heavy agenda items, large groups and obvious status gaps, and wrong when the goal is warming a room or building shared ownership — for that, talk.",
