            en: "Rational choice looks only to future costs and benefits: a sunk cost is neither recoverable nor changed by what you now decide, so it should be ignored. People persistently fail this—loss aversion, escalation of commitment and self-justification coax them into \"I've come this far\" and \"so much already invested,\" doubling down until they are in deeper, from doomed projects and failed R&D to positions they refuse to close. The canonical case is the Concorde: economics long since unfavorable, yet each partner kept funding. The defense in management is not willpower but institutional design—a stop-loss line and exit criteria set in advance, decision-makers separated somewhat from those who sank the cost, and an independent review asking \"knowing what we know, would we start this today?\"",
