            en: "SCAMPER is the idea checklist Eberle distilled from the brainstorming in Osborn's Applied Imagination (1953) into seven operations you can run item by item. It splits a vague \"think of something new\" into seven moves: Substitute (swap a material, part or person), Combine (fuse two things into one), Adapt (borrow a way of doing it from elsewhere), Modify (magnify, shrink or alter an attribute), Put-to-other-uses (find a wholly new use), Eliminate (cut what is redundant) and Reverse (flip the order or swap the roles). Its worth is that it hands a stuck person a trigger — no need to wait for a flash, just sweep the seven and a direction usually surfaces — and it turns a team's idea session from free-associating into a structured pass across a whole product line. It works best when you first name the exact thing to improve, then force yourself one concrete variation per letter.",
