            zh: "第一性原理的思维法可追到亚里士多德、因马斯克而重新出名：面对问题不用“类比”（别人都这么做、历来如此），而是追到物理与成本的最底层事实——比如“电池组贵”其实贵在成品形态，拆到底不过是几种金属材料的组合，按现货价买原料再自己组合远没那么贵——然后在不被历史惯例束缚的地基上重新往上推导。它是颠覆性创新的引擎，能跳出“行业惯例”这堵看不见的墙。但也要诚实：它极其耗脑力，并非事事都该从零推导；日常运营用启发式（成熟套路、类比、最佳实践）快得多，只有当你面对关键取舍、或一个“看似不可能”的难题、或整个行业都默认却没人验证过的前提时，才值得动用第一性原理去重新发问。",
            en: "First-principles thinking traces to Aristotle and was revived by Musk: rather than reason by analogy (everyone does it this way, it has always been so), dig down to the bedrock physical and cost facts — a battery pack is expensive as a product, but at bottom it is just a handful of metal commodities, far cheaper bought raw and recombined — then re-derive upward on a footing unshackled by convention. It is an engine of disruptive innovation, able to vault the invisible wall of \"industry practice\". But be honest: it is costly in thought and no task should be derived from zero; heuristics — mature playbooks, analogy, best practice — run daily operations far faster, and first principles earn their keep only on pivotal choices, on problems that look impossible, or on premises the whole field assumes yet no one has tested.",
