            en: "Ries carried Toyota's lean thinking into the chaos of a startup: instead of perfecting a product in a cave for years and then betting everything on one big launch, break every business idea into a chain of assumptions to be tested and run build-measure-learn loops with an MVP — the least thing that starts a learning cycle — letting real user data, not intuition, do the judging. Validated learning is the only asset that reliably accrues from the process; when the metrics keep showing the path is dead, pivot decisively, and when they hold, persevere or double down. The order is fixed: first prove, at the smallest cost, that the problem and the demand are real, only then talk about scaling, burning cash into insight rather than into inventory. It reshaped how startups and inside incubators build, yet the risks are as real: an MVP so rough it damages the brand and early trust, or a cult of speed that, without a long-term vision, becomes directionless thrashing.",
