            zh: "塔勒布把事物对波动的反应分成三类：脆弱的（怕波动、一压就碎，如玻璃）、稳健的（扛得住、大致不变，如石头）、反脆弱的（越折腾越强，如免疫系统、锻炼后的肌肉、广撒网的创业组合）。反脆弱的结构根源是“凸性”——下行亏损有限、上行收益敞开，于是适度的压力、试错、随机性与多样性反而成了它的养料。管理启示是别把系统过度优化到僵化：消除一切小波动看似高效，实则把风险攒到最后一次总爆发，如只涨不跌的市场、去掉全部冗余的供应链。更好的做法是保留选择权、小步试错、从失败中汲取信息，并用“杠铃策略”把大部分资源放在安全处、少部分押在高凸性的赌注上。它与韧性不同：韧性是“不被击垮”，反脆弱是“因被击打而变得更好”。",
            en: "Taleb sorts things by how they meet volatility: the fragile (harmed by it, shatters under pressure, like glass), the robust (shrugs it off and stays much the same, like a stone) and the antifragile (gets stronger for it, like the immune system, a muscle after training, or a startup portfolio spread across many bets). The structural root of antifragility is convexity, a capped downside and an open upside, so measured stress, trial and error, randomness and diversity become its food rather than its threat. The managerial lesson is not to over-optimise a system into rigidity: removing every small shock looks efficient but stores risk up for one final blow-up, as in markets that only rise or supply chains stripped of all slack. Better to keep optionality, tinker in small steps, harvest information from failure, and use a barbell that parks most resources in safety while placing a little on highly convex bets. Unlike resilience, which resists being broken, antifragility gets better from being struck.",
