            en: "The decision matrix — also called a Pugh matrix or scoring model — is an old tool for making a multi-option trade-off transparent. First list the criteria you genuinely care about (cost, quality, risk, time, fit), assign each a weight, then score every option item by item, multiply by the weight and sum, and finally rank by total. It converts a talk-past-each-other contest of loud voices into a table of numbers the whole group can put on the desk and interrogate together — simple and visual, well suited to choosing a supplier, a technology or a project priority. But mind the traps: the weights already smuggle in values and bias, the basis for a score may differ from person to person, and the weighted sum feigns a precision down to the decimal built on rough estimates at input. It structures your thinking and forces your standards into the open, yet does not make the real value judgements for you — the table is a decision's assistant, not its master.",
