            en: "The method (Kelley and Walker, for a DuPont turnaround in 1957) wires tasks into a network by precedence, computes earliest starts forwards and latest starts backwards, and calls the chain with zero total float the critical path. It hands the manager a counter-intuitive order: to finish earlier you may only act on tasks in that chain, moving resource from slack work into it — not 'help wherever looks busy', which makes non-critical work prettier and the completion date unchanged. Two riders. The critical path shifts: compress it and the next-longest chain takes over, since multiple and near-critical paths are normal in real projects, so recompute weekly. And three-point estimates (optimistic, most likely, pessimistic — PERT) describe single task durations, while the probability of finishing the project depends on the whole path: a plan built by scheduling every task at its 'most likely' duration frequently lands under fifty percent on time. That is one piece of arithmetic behind Brooks's law.",
