            en: "Supply chain management coordinates purchasing, production, warehousing, logistics and information across firm boundaries, stated like a slogan: the right quantity, at the right time, in the right place. Its signature finding is the bullwhip (Forrester 1961, under the name demand amplification): a demand signal is magnified stage by stage as it travels upstream, through over-forecasting, batch ordering, price-driven panic stocking and double ordering during shortages — the Pampers-diaper case, small swing at retail and a large one at the factory, has become the canonical illustration. The remedies all push toward transparency and shortness: share point-of-sale data, cut lead time (more fundamental than squeezing inventory), vendor-managed inventory, and postponement, deferring differentiation as late as possible. Add the reality that arrived in the 2020s: the objective function changed. Optimising turnover and cost alone stretches the chain so taut that one break stops everything, hence the current framing of efficiency as base and resilience as ceiling — multi-sourcing, nearshoring and safety stock for critical parts are not waste but insurance against a low-probability event, and the premium should actually be computed before it is bought.",
