            en: "5S comes from Toyota (seiri, seiton, seiso, seiketsu, shitsuke—five Japanese words each starting with \"S\") as lean's most basic entry discipline: clear out what is unneeded (sort), give every item a fixed, obvious place (set in order), keep things clean and use cleaning to surface anomalies (shine), institutionalize the first three into standards (standardize), and sustain it by habit and self-discipline (sustain). It looks like spring-cleaning but is really the ground work that makes problems visible at a glance and cuts the waste of searching and carrying—and a touchstone for whether a team can hold small disciplines. Hospitals adopting 5S cut medication errors and infections; offices apply it to reclaim time lost hunting for files and tools. Its point is not one deep clean but freezing an order that reveals anomalies and keeping it for the long haul.",
