            en: "Value-stream mapping comes from the Toyota system and was codified and spread by Rother and Shook in Learning to See: tracing both the material and the information flow, it draws every step in turn, noting cycle time, batch, changeover, work-in-process and waiting, to yield two starkly contrasting figures — the total lead time from order to delivery, and the value-added time that is typically only a sliver of it (often under a percent). The map's power is turning waste from a slogan into a ratio you can see, so the team designs an ideal future-state map and lays out an improvement path and priority toward it. It is called lean's diagnostic X-ray, used alongside kanban (pull on demand), the theory of constraints (find the bottleneck first) and the value chain (read profit across the whole arc), so as not to optimise one local step while slowing the flow of the whole.",
