            en: "The Italian economist Pareto observed that about eighty percent of his country's wealth sat with twenty percent of its people; Joseph Juran distilled this pervasive imbalance into the eighty-twenty rule (the vital few and the trivial many) and brought it into quality management: most defects stem from few causes, most sales from few customers, most failures from few steps, most complaints from few problems. It is not a precise ratio but a mindset that input and output are inherently uneven and imbalance is the norm, a warning not to spread effort evenly over everything. With a Pareto chart — bars ranked high-to-low overlaid by a cumulative-percentage curve — you can separate the vital few at a glance and point scarce resources at what truly moves. It is the shared logic behind prioritisation, ABC inventory classification, root-cause work and every put-first-things-first advice, though one must guard against ignoring the long tail and systemic causes by over-focusing on the head.",
