            en: "There are two basic bases of accounting. Cash basis records only at the moment money actually moves — simple and close to whether the pocket truly holds cash, but it warps performance to the settlement date: goods sold this month count as revenue only when paid next month, and outlays already made can spare the current book if payment is dragged to year-end while the owing lurks underneath. Accrual basis records by the period an item belongs to: revenue is recognised when it is earned, expense when it is incurred to earn that revenue, the two matched in the same period; it is this logic that spawns receivables, payables, advances received, prepayments and deferred charges, so that profit better reflects the real effort of a stretch of operating. The price is heavy reliance on estimates and judgement (bad-debt rates, depreciation lives, revenue-recognition timing) and the room it leaves to pull revenue forward and defer cost. Hence to read a firm one must set the income statement against the cash-flow statement: the accrual view shows whether you earned, the cash view shows whether the money arrived — profit that long fails to turn into cash is often a warning sign.",
