            zh: "利润是账面意见，现金是事实，而两家赚钱的公司可能一家现金紧绷一家现金泛滥——差别就在营运资本。它管的是三类被占用的钱：存货压在仓里、应收挂在客户名下、应付欠着供应商。现金转换周期把占用量化：存货天数加应收天数减应付天数。快时尚把存货天数从九十天压到三十天，释放的不是效率而是可支配现金。零售巨头的经典玩法是把应付天数做长、收现做快，靠负营运资本让供应商变相提供免息融资——但把上游现金流压到极限会把生态压碎，供应商账期拖欠在多数国家有法律与声誉代价。反向的坑是存货“安全垫”：为防断供层层加码，占用的资金最终都计进了成本。所以这条线上真正的本事是区分流动性保险与资金浪费，并且明白一件事：缩短转换周期释放的现金，常常比多赚一单来得更快也更确定。",
            en: "Profit is an opinion, cash is a fact, and two profitable firms can differ utterly — one cash-tight, one flooding — the gap living in working capital. It manages three kinds of money held hostage: inventory sitting in the warehouse, receivables parked at customers, payables owed to suppliers. The cash-conversion cycle quantifies the hostage-taking: days of inventory plus days of receivables minus days of payables. When fast fashion compressed inventory from ninety days to thirty, what was freed was not efficiency but spendable cash. The classic retail play lengthens payables while collecting instantly, running on negative working capital so suppliers effectively lend at zero interest — yet squeeze upstream to the limit and the ecosystem cracks, since late payment carries legal and reputational cost in most jurisdictions. The mirror trap is the inventory cushion: stacking safety stock against every conceivable shortage, then finding the tied-up capital has been quietly booked as cost. The skill on this line is telling liquidity insurance from wasted money, and holding one fact clearly: cash released by shortening the cycle usually arrives faster and surer than cash from one more sale.",
