            en: "Popularized by Joel Stern and G. Bennett Stewart, economic value added equals after-tax operating profit minus (the cost of capital × capital employed). Its sharpness is the reminder that positive accounting profit is not the same as value creation—if the return falls short of capital's opportunity cost, the books show a gain while the economics show a loss and shareholders are quietly eroded. It forces managers to answer for every dollar of capital tied up, curbing blind expansion and \"growth for growth's sake,\" and is often used to design executive pay and internal scorecards closer to value; Cisco made it famous as a company-wide operating compass in the 1990s. Critics note it still leans on accounting assumptions, can be gamed by the eyeballed cost-of-capital rate, and sits awkwardly with startups and long-cycle industries—at bottom it is an accounting proxy for free cash flow.",
