            en: "Opportunity cost is the value of the best alternative you must give up to take a choice: it never books as an expense and raises no invoice, yet it decides every trade-off — if cash spent building an in-house team would earn more poured back into the core business, that more is the real hidden cost of building in-house, and the owner's time supervising a line is priced by the value they would create elsewhere, often far above the wage of hiring someone. It also warns that money already spent is a sunk cost that should not sway future decisions, and that running every resource at full tilt is not the same as using resources well. In decision trees, investment appraisal and allocation, making the invisible runner-up explicit keeps you from fooling yourself that a choice costs nothing — there is no free lunch, and every unit of time, capital and attention you commit carries its own price.",
