          detail: {
            zh: "折旧是把一项固定资产（厂房、设备、车辆）的购置成本，按其预计使用年限与残值，用系统方法分摊到各期费用，以体现“资产随使用而损耗、其价值随服务一点点流出”。常见有直线法（各年均摊）与加速折旧法（前几年多提，以匹配技术更新快或争取税法优惠）。它有三个常被误读之处：折旧是费用却不必当期付现，所以利润与现金流会分道扬镳；它既影响账面利润也影响税基；它提醒管理者设备的“成本”不只在买入那天，而贯穿整个服役期。与之并列、针对无形资产（专利、商誉、软件）的对应概念叫“摊销”。理解折旧，才能读懂利润为何低于现金，以及重资产行业的利润为何对折旧假设异常敏感。",
            en: "Depreciation spreads the purchase cost of a fixed asset — a plant, a machine, a vehicle — over its expected useful life and residual value by a systematic method, to reflect that the asset wears out and its value flows out bit by bit as service. The usual forms are straight-line (an even charge each year) and accelerated (front-loading the expense to match fast obsolescence or a tax break). Three points are often misread: depreciation is an expense that need not be paid out in cash, so profit and cash flow part ways; it shapes reported profit and the tax base alike; and it reminds managers that an asset's cost is not just the day it is bought but runs through its whole service life. Its counterpart for intangible assets — patents, goodwill, software — is amortisation. Only by grasping depreciation do you see why profit can sit below cash, and why capital-intensive industries are so sensitive to the depreciation assumption.",
          },
          tags: [{ zh: "财务", en: "Finance" }],
          related: ["income-statement", "three-statements", "time-value"],
