            en: "Conventional budgeting almost always tinkers on last year's figures — add a few points or trim a few — and so quietly ossifies past waste and inefficiency into an inertia of \"we had it last year, so we should have it again.\" Peter Pyhrr's zero-based budgeting instead resets to zero every period: no activity may plead \"it's always been this way,\" and each must argue from scratch what resources it needs and whether it deserves a place near the front of the priority queue, after which the budget is redistributed by cost-benefit. It is strong at wringing out legacy bloat and shifting resources from auto-renewing old programmes toward more worthwhile new ones. The cost is plain, too: re-justifying everything from zero is time-consuming and heavy for operations that are otherwise steady, which is why in practice it is used for expense clean-ups, major turnarounds or periodic reset scrutiny rather than run in full every single year.",
