            en: "Splitting costs by behaviour is the starting point of a whole family of analysis: fixed costs (rent, monthly salaries, equipment depreciation) stay put within a capacity range, while variable costs (materials, piece-pay, freight, sales commission) rise and fall near-proportionally with output. The cut yields several key judgements — operating leverage (the higher the fixed share, the more violently a small sales swing is amplified into profit, for joy or for pain); unit economics (how much contribution each extra sale leaves once its variable cost is taken out, to go cover the fixed); and the breakeven point, fixed cost divided by contribution per unit. Pricing, scaling and make-or-buy all begin by asking whether this money is fixed or variable: the heavier the fixed outlay, the higher the leverage and the risk, but once the balance point is crossed, the reward from scale is all the greater.",
