            en: "Debt is relatively cheap (interest is tax-deductible and creditors demand a lower return than shareholders) but must be repaid on schedule, amplifies downside risk, and can push a firm into financial distress or bankruptcy; equity carries no hard obligation to repay and can absorb losses, yet it costs more and earns no tax shield. Under a set of ideal assumptions (no taxes, no bankruptcy cost, symmetric information) Modigliani and Miller proved capital structure irrelevant to value, while in reality tax shields, distress costs, agency problems and managerial motives break that neutrality into a trade-off theory: moderate debt lifts value through the shield until, past a tipping point, the risk cost bites back and value falls. Leverage thus magnifies shareholder return and loss alike, and can breed crisis; sound firms usually stop short of the supposed optimal ratio and deliberately keep spare borrowing capacity, so they hold ammunition for a lean season or a sudden opportunity.",
