            en: "Amy Edmondson found the best teams are not error-free but bold enough to put errors on the table, so they learn faster. Psychological safety is not lowered standards nor false harmony — it exists precisely so that hard truths have somewhere to land; her formula is mutual respect plus candour. It also topped Google's Project Aristotle, which studied scores of teams, as the single strongest factor in effectiveness. The trap to avoid is reading safety as nobody-is-held-accountable: safety means daring to report an error, ask a dumb question, admit I do not know yet — standards still apply, only accountability aims at the process, not the person. The lever is the leader, not the personality: admit your own fallibility first (I am not sure either), ask more and judge less, and when bad news lands thank the messenger before tracing the cause — because staff watch what happens to the person who brings bad news, not what the poster says. The real danger sign is not dissent but a quiet room where no one speaks.",
