          oneLiner: {
            zh: "目标—反馈—评估—发展闭环;一年一度的打分正被持续对话取代。",
            en: "A loop of goals, feedback, appraisal and development — the annual rating gives way to ongoing conversation.",
          },
          detail: {
            zh: "考核同时担三件事：对齐目标（承接 MBO 与 OKR）、发展人与分配回报、为人事决定留依据——三者挤在一张表里，正是问题之源。认知偏差的清单很整齐：近因效应把九月到十二月当成全年，光环效应让一个亮点抹平其余，宽大化让主管把所有人都评到中上以回避对话，序列位置效应让最后一个谈的人吃点小亏。比偏差更难治的是机制：一旦排名与奖金挂钩，自评与互评都会变战略行为，同事之间也会从协作转向防守。因此行业的修法不是把年度评估删掉（完全去掉后员工对反馈质量的焦虑反而上升），而是拆频率：目标季度校准、进展双向对话、发展话题单独拎出不与分档同场——谷歌 2020 年后就把评估与薪酬拆开。但要提醒：任何拆频率的修法都依赖主管真能给出高质量反馈，而这本身是需要训练与投入的东西。",
            en: "A review carries three jobs at once: aligning goals (inheriting MBO and OKR), developing people while allocating reward, and leaving a record for personnel decisions — squeezed into one form, which is the source of most of its troubles. The bias list is tidy: recency mistakes September-to-December for the year, the halo lets one bright spot flatten the rest, leniency has managers rating everyone above average to avoid the conversation, and order effects short-change whoever is discussed last. Harder to fix than bias is mechanics: once ranking is wired to bonus, self-assessment and peer input both become strategic acts, and colleagues start defending rather than collaborating. So the industry's repair is not deleting the annual review — where fully removed, employees' anxiety about feedback quality rises — but splitting frequency: quarterly goal calibration, two-way conversations about progress, and development topics carried to a separate room from the rating. Google did exactly that split in 2020. The caveat holds: every frequency split presumes managers can actually deliver usable feedback, which is itself a skill requiring training and protected time.",
          },
          tags: [{ zh: "人际", en: "Relations" }],
          related: ["goal-setting", "equity", "drucker-mbo"],
