          id: "bases-power",
          icon: "🗝️",
          term: { zh: "权力的五种基础", en: "Five Bases of Power" },
          level: 3,
          oneLiner: {
            zh: "法定、奖赏、强制、专家、参照——影响力可来自职位,也可来自能力与人格。",
            en: "Legitimate, reward, coercive, expert and referent — influence can come from the chair, or from skill and character.",
          },
          detail: {
            zh: "弗伦奇与雷文 1959 年指出权力不止是职位授权，而分五种来源：法定（你的头衔有权命令）、奖酬（能给好处）、强制（能罚）、专家（你懂得多）、参照（别人信你、愿意追随）。前三种都挂在岗位上，人走权即消；专家权与参照权长在个人身上，往往比奖惩与强制更持久、更少抵触。管理者的隐痛正在这条缝：只有法定权力、缺专业与信任时，指令会层层打折——表面服从、实际拖延。所以真正的权威多来自后两种，而这也是为什么新空降的高管头几个月拼命展现专业、而不是忙着立威。用五把尺还能自查权力的成色：你让人做事，靠的是他不能不做（强制）、做了有糖（奖酬），还是他真想跟你干（参照）？越依赖前两把，组织越贵越脆；越靠后两把，越少监督成本。",
            en: "French and Raven, in 1959, argued power is more than the office and splits into five bases: legitimate (your title may command), reward (you can hand out benefits), coercive (you can punish), expert (you know more), referent (others trust and want to follow you). The first three hang on the post and vanish when the person leaves; expert and referent power grow on the individual and usually outlast and draw less resistance than reward or coercion. The manager's hidden ache lies exactly in that seam: with only legitimate power and little expertise or trust, commands get discounted layer by layer — outward compliance, actual delay. So lasting authority leans on the last two, which is why a newly arrived executive spends the first months showing competence rather than asserting rank. The five bases also serve as a self-assay of power's quality: when you get someone to act, is it because they must not (coercion), because there is sugar for it (reward), or because they genuinely want to follow you (referent)? The more you lean on the first two, the costlier and more brittle the organisation; the more on the last two, the less supervision it needs.",
          },
