          term: { zh: "RIA 阅读法", en: "The RIA Reading Method" },
          level: 2,
          oneLiner: {
            zh: "阅读—重述—拆为己用：把书里的知识变成“我自己的下一步行动”，而非止于读完。",
            en: "Read, Interpret, Appropriate — turn a book's idea into 'my own next action' rather than stopping at finishing it.",
          },
          detail: {
            zh: "RIA 阅读法由赵周在《这样读书就够了》里提出，面向成人“拆书”式学习：R（阅读）阅读原文片段、I（重述）用自己的话重述含义、A（内化）拆为己用——A1 联系自身过往经验、A2 规划具体可执行的下一步应用。它针的是“读了就忘、用不出来”的痛点：成人学习不为积累知识而为改变行为，把读书从被动输入变成行动设计。它常与便签法、拆书帮与学习型组织结合，适合致用类、方法论书，对文学与理论经典则未必合适。它的要点不在快读，而在把一段话接回自己的经验与行动里。说到底，它把“读完没”改成“用到没”来衡量学习，正与成人教育以问题为中心、以应用为归宿的理念相合。",
            en: "The RIA method, set out by Zhao Zhou in his reading-for-adults book, is a \"book-dismantling\" approach aimed at adult learning: R (Reading) reads a passage, I (Interpretation) restates its meaning in your own words, and A (Appropriation) makes it yours—A1 links it to your past experience, A2 plans a concrete next action to apply it. It targets the ache of \"read it, forget it, never use it\": adults learn not to bank knowledge but to change behavior, so reading turns from passive intake into action design. It pairs often with the sticky-note method, book-club \"dismantlers\" and the learning organization, suiting how-to and practical titles far better than literary or theoretical classics. Its point is not speed-reading but threading a passage back into your own experience and next step.",
          },
