          term: { zh: "职业锚", en: "Career Anchors" },
          level: 3,
          oneLiner: {
            zh: "每个人最深处的职业选择“锚点”不同:技术、管理、自主、安全、创业、服务或挑战。",
            en: "Everyone has a deepest career anchor — technical, managerial, autonomy, security, entrepreneurial, service or challenge.",
          },
          detail: {
            zh: "施恩基于长期跟踪麻省理工毕业生发现：人到一定阶段，会在实践中逐渐形成“职业锚”——即无论如何都不愿放弃的核心价值观、动机与才干，它像船锚一样把人稳定在某一类工作上。他归纳出常见八类：技术或功能能力型（追求在本专业做到顶尖）、通用管理能力型（追求统筹与整合）、自主或独立型（追求不被约束、自己定规则）、安全或稳定型（追求可预期与保障）、创业型（追求创造属于自己的事业）、服务或献身型（追求某一使命与价值）、挑战型（追求攻克越难越好的难题）以及生活平衡型（追求工作与生活整体协调）。对管理者的意义在于：别把一位技术顶尖的人硬推向其“锚点”之外的管理岗——那往往同时失去一个好专家、得到一个差经理，正与彼得原理相呼应；也更该帮每个人认清并顺应自己的锚，在合适的通道上成长。",
            en: "Schein, tracking MIT graduates over years, found that as people work they settle on a career anchor — the core values, motives and talents they will not give up, which hold them steady on a certain kind of work like an anchor on a ship. He set out eight common types: technical or functional competence (mastery of a discipline to its top), general managerial competence (integration and running things), autonomy or independence (not being bound, setting one's own rules), security or stability (predictability and assurance), entrepreneurial creation (building something that is one's own), service or dedication (a calling and a set of values), pure challenge (tackling the harder problem), and lifestyle integration (a whole life balanced around work). Its managerial meaning is: do not push a technical star into a management post their anchor rejects — you often lose a good expert and gain a poor manager, a direct tie-in to the Peter principle; and help each person see and follow their own anchor, growing along the track that fits.",
          },
          tags: [{ zh: "职业", en: "Career" }],
